<p class="gna-lede">Whether braces alone can correct an adult underbite depends mainly on whether the problem lies in the position of the teeth or in the jaw bones themselves. Mild underbites, and some moderate ones, can often be disguised with orthodontics alone, while a significant mismatch between the jaws usually needs braces combined with corrective jaw surgery. The right choice for you can only be made after an examination, X-rays and a joint discussion between an orthodontist and a maxillofacial surgeon.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-an-underbite">What is an underbite?</h2>
<p>In a typical bite, the upper front teeth sit slightly in front of the lower front teeth when you close. In an underbite, the lower front teeth sit in front of the upper ones, or meet them edge to edge. Dentists call this a <strong>Class III malocclusion</strong>. It may involve only the front teeth, or extend to the back teeth as a crossbite, where the lower teeth close outside the upper teeth.</p>
<p>An underbite can come from several different problems:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A lower jaw that has grown too far forward</strong> (mandibular prognathism), which often runs in families.</li>
  <li><strong>An upper jaw that is underdeveloped</strong> (maxillary hypoplasia), which makes the middle of the face look flat. This is a frequent contributor and is common in people born with a cleft lip and palate.</li>
  <li><strong>A combination of both</strong>, which is very common.</li>
  <li><strong>Tooth position alone</strong>: upper front teeth tipped backwards or lower front teeth tipped forwards, with jaws that are broadly in proportion.</li>
  <li><strong>A functional shift</strong> (sometimes called pseudo-Class III), where the lower jaw slides forward on closing because the front teeth meet awkwardly first.</li>
</ul>
<p>People seek help for different reasons: difficulty biting through food with the front teeth, chewing mainly on the back teeth, wear or chipping of the front teeth, a lisp on certain sounds, lips that feel strained, or unhappiness with a prominent chin or flat midface. Whether an underbite causes jaw-joint pain is debated; the evidence linking the two is weak.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>An underbite (Class III bite) can be caused by the teeth, the jaws, or both.</li>
    <li>Braces alone can disguise a mild-to-moderate problem by tilting teeth, but they do not change the size or position of the jaw bones.</li>
    <li>Jaw surgery repositions the bones themselves and is usually considered for moderate-to-severe skeletal underbites.</li>
    <li>Surgery is planned only after facial growth has finished, which is often later in young men than in young women.</li>
    <li>Both routes involve orthodontics and both carry risks; the right one depends on your diagnosis and your priorities.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="teeth-or-jaws">Is the problem in your teeth or your jaws?</h2>
<p>This is the single most important question, and it cannot be answered by looking in a mirror. At an assessment, the orthodontist and surgeon will usually:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Examine your face from the front and the side, at rest and while smiling, to see whether the upper jaw, lower jaw, chin or all three contribute.</li>
  <li>Check whether you can bring your front teeth edge to edge. If you can, part of the underbite may be a functional shift rather than a bone problem.</li>
  <li>Take a <strong>lateral cephalogram</strong> (a standardised side X-ray of the skull) and measure the angles between the upper jaw, the lower jaw and the base of the skull.</li>
  <li>Take a panoramic X-ray, and often a cone-beam CT (CBCT) scan, to look at the tooth roots, the thickness of bone around the lower front teeth, the jaw joints and any asymmetry.</li>
  <li>Record digital scans or impressions of your teeth and clinical photographs.</li>
</ul>
<p>Two people with similar-looking bites can have very different underlying problems. One may have well-proportioned jaws with tipped teeth; another may have teeth that have already tilted a long way to compensate for a large jaw imbalance. That second person has much less room left for orthodontics to work with.</p>

<h2 id="braces-alone">What can braces alone achieve?</h2>
<p>Treating a skeletal underbite with orthodontics alone is called <strong>camouflage</strong>. The jaws stay where they are; the teeth are moved to hide the mismatch. Typically the upper front teeth are tilted forwards and the lower front teeth are tilted backwards until they overlap normally. Depending on the case, this may involve:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Fixed braces (metal or ceramic) or, in selected milder cases, clear aligners</li>
  <li>Removing teeth, often lower premolars, to create space for the lower front teeth to move back</li>
  <li>Elastics (small rubber bands) between the upper and lower teeth</li>
  <li><strong>Temporary anchorage devices (TADs)</strong>: small titanium mini-screws placed in the bone under local anaesthesia, which help move the whole lower row of teeth backwards</li>
</ul>
<p>Camouflage can work well when the skeletal difference is mild to moderate, the face is broadly balanced, and there is enough bone around the lower front teeth for them to move safely. It avoids a general anaesthetic and a hospital stay.</p>
<p>Its limits are equally important. Because the bones are not moved, the profile changes only slightly, and a prominent chin or flat midface largely remains. Tilting the lower front teeth back too far can thin the bone and gum around them and cause gum recession. Treatment often takes two years or more, and teeth held in compensated positions need long-term retainers. A recent review of camouflage methods in adults found that most published evidence comes from case reports and small series, so outcomes are less predictable than many people assume.</p>

<h2 id="when-jaw-surgery">When is jaw surgery the better option?</h2>
<p>Orthodontics combined with <a href="/orthognathic-surgery">orthognathic (corrective jaw) surgery</a> is usually recommended when:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The difference between the jaws is moderate to severe on X-ray measurements</li>
  <li>The lower front teeth have already tipped back as far as is safe, or the bone around them is thin</li>
  <li>The upper jaw is clearly underdeveloped, or the jaw is asymmetrical</li>
  <li>There is also an open bite or a significant crossbite at the back</li>
  <li>Your main concern is the facial profile, not only the bite</li>
</ul>
<p>The surgery is done under general anaesthesia, usually through incisions inside the mouth, so there are normally no scars on the face. Depending on the diagnosis, the surgeon may move the upper jaw forward (a <strong>Le Fort I osteotomy</strong>), move the lower jaw back (a <strong>bilateral sagittal split osteotomy</strong>), do both together (bimaxillary or "double jaw" surgery), and sometimes reshape the chin. The bones are held in their new positions with small titanium plates and screws, so the jaws are generally not wired shut. Most people stay in hospital for one to three nights.</p>
<p>The orthodontic part is different from camouflage. Before surgery, braces are used to <em>undo</em> the natural tooth compensation, placing the teeth upright over the bone. This often makes the underbite look worse for a while, which is expected and is what allows the surgeon to correct the jaws fully. After surgery, a shorter phase of orthodontics fine-tunes the bite. In selected cases a "surgery-first" approach is used, with most of the orthodontic work done afterwards. Computer-based <a href="/blogs/role-of-3d-virtual-planning/">3D virtual surgical planning</a> helps the team plan the jaw movements precisely and show you a simulation of the expected change, although real results always vary from person to person.</p>

<h2 id="growth">Why growth must be finished first</h2>
<p>The lower jaw is one of the last parts of the face to stop growing. In girls, facial growth usually slows by the mid-teens; in boys, the lower jaw can keep growing into the late teens or early twenties. If jaw surgery is done while growth is still active, the lower jaw may continue to move forward and the underbite can return.</p>
<p>For this reason surgeons look for evidence that growth has stopped before operating, for example by comparing measurements on X-rays taken six to twelve months apart, assessing skeletal maturity on the cephalogram, and checking that the bite has stopped changing. Age alone is not a reliable guide.</p>
<p>In children who are still growing, the picture is different. Early orthodontic treatment, such as an appliance that encourages the upper jaw forward, can help some younger patients, particularly those with an underdeveloped upper jaw, although some will still need surgery as adults. If your child's lower front teeth bite in front of the upper ones, an early orthodontic opinion is worthwhile.</p>

