<h2 id="comparison">Braces alone vs braces with surgery</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Braces alone (camouflage)</th><th>Braces with jaw surgery</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>What moves</td><td>Teeth only</td><td>Jaw bones, with teeth fine-tuned by orthodontics</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Usually suits</td><td>Dental or mild-to-moderate skeletal underbite</td><td>Moderate-to-severe skeletal underbite, asymmetry, flat midface</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Effect on profile</td><td>Small</td><td>Can be significant</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Anaesthesia and stay</td><td>None for braces; local anaesthesia for mini-screws</td><td>General anaesthesia; usually one to three nights in hospital</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Typical overall timeline</td><td>Often 18 to 30 months</td><td>Often 18 months to 3 years, including both orthodontic phases</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Main drawbacks</td><td>Gum recession risk, limited change to the face, long-term retainers</td><td>Surgical risks, swelling, numbness, time away from work or college</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Insurance</td><td>Usually an out-patient expense</td><td>May be covered when medically indicated, subject to policy terms and pre-authorisation</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="risks-recovery">Risks and recovery: an honest picture</h2>
<p>No option is free of trade-offs, and it helps to know them before you choose.</p>
<h3>With orthodontics alone</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Gum recession or thin bone around the lower front teeth</li>
  <li>Some shortening of tooth roots (root resorption), usually minor</li>
  <li>Relapse if retainers are not worn as advised</li>
  <li>A bite that is improved but a profile that is largely unchanged</li>
</ul>
<h3>With jaw surgery</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Swelling and bruising that peak around the second or third day and settle substantially over two to three weeks, with subtle swelling lasting several months</li>
  <li>Numbness of the lower lip and chin after lower jaw surgery, because the nerve that supplies sensation runs through the jaw. Feeling improves for most people over months, but some altered sensation can persist.</li>
  <li>Numbness of the upper gum and palate after upper jaw surgery, usually temporary</li>
  <li>Bleeding, infection, or problems with plates or screws, occasionally needing their removal later</li>
  <li>Some relapse of the jaw position, needing further orthodontic adjustment</li>
  <li>Jaw-joint symptoms, which may improve, stay the same or occasionally worsen</li>
</ul>
<p>Recovery involves a liquid and then soft diet for several weeks; our practical guide on <a href="/blogs/what-to-eat-after-jaw-surgery/">what to eat after jaw surgery</a> covers this step by step. Many people return to desk work or college in two to three weeks, but recovery varies, and the final result is usually judged six to twelve months after surgery. Our <a href="/blogs/recovery-time-after-jaw-surgery-complete-healing-timeline-and-care-tips/">jaw surgery recovery timeline</a> explains each stage in more detail.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-a-specialist">When to see a specialist, and how the decision is made</h2>
<p>It is worth having an assessment if you:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Cannot bite through food with your front teeth, or chew mainly on one side</li>
  <li>Notice your front teeth wearing, chipping or becoming loose</li>
  <li>Had orthodontic treatment as a teenager and the underbite has come back</li>
  <li>Have difficulty with certain speech sounds, jaw discomfort, or feel self-conscious about your profile</li>
  <li>Have a child whose lower front teeth bite in front of the upper ones</li>
</ul>
<p>At Gnathos Facial, our surgeons review your records together with your orthodontist. We explain what is achievable with braces alone, what surgery would add, and what each route involves in time, risk and recovery. For some people, camouflage is a reasonable choice; for others, it would mean accepting a compromise in function or appearance. You make the decision with the full picture in front of you, and if you choose surgery it is carried out at NABH-accredited partner hospitals in Hyderabad.</p>
<p>If your concern is a lopsided rather than a protruding jaw, our article on <a href="/blogs/can-braces-fix-an-asymmetrical-jaw/">whether braces can fix an asymmetrical jaw</a> explains how the same principles apply.</p>
